(6947061, 1046448, 137576, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by PW321:</b></div><hr/><i> Interesting concept but I''m not sure the execution was 100%. The "walker" isn''t actually walking and the brolly doesn''t fit the sunset ... no rain ... love the night cityscape. Would like to see this one done with an actual walking figure striding over the landscape which is the image your title and most of your picture suggests to me. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This is clearly shot in the dark, that''s light pollution from the city. There''s no sun setting and it was raining, and again I was walking as its a 10 second exposure I just paused longer in the middle to get some stronger definition of the silhouette<br/> <br/><a href="//www.flickr.com/photos/cyberprop/sets/72157632348627546/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.flickr.com/photos/cyberprop/sets/72157632348627546/</a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2013-01-02 10:07:52.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by PW321: Interesting concept but I''m not sure the execution was 100%. The "walker" isn''t actually walking and the brolly doesn''t fit the sunset ... no rain ... love the night cityscape. Would like to see this one done with an actual walking figure striding over the landscape which is the image your title and most of your picture suggests to me.   This is clearly shot in the dark, that''s light pollution from the city. There''s no sun setting and it was raining, and again I was walking as its a 10 second exposure I just paused longer in the middle to get some stronger definition of the silhouette //www.flickr.com/photos/cyberprop/sets/72157632348627546/', '2013-01-02 01:52:06.000000', '2013-01-02 10:07:52.000000', 0),
(6947095, 1046382, 155989, '<td valign="top"><i>if anyone felt offended</i> <br/> <br/>not offended but my dislike of guns does affect my interaction with and interpretation of the picture. if photography is viewed as art and not just a technical process of capturing an image, then it isn''t just about the technical aspects of the photo but how the viewer interacts with it as well. <br/> <br/>I think that there are a few examples in the comments below of how the opinions of the viewer affect the interaction. <br/> <br/>You wanted to convey the good guy "sheriff" holding his ground against the outlaws but more than a few comments below show a completely different interpretation despite your title. It is described as ''ominous'', ''menacing'', I used ''vigilante'' and others referenced "Deliverance". This would indicate that on some level with some viewers your image failed to convey a ''good guy'' impression. Perhaps the absence of a clear ''bad guy'' in the image contributes as well as the fact that your character is wearing a black hat which image is well embedded in cowboy symbolism as belonging to the ''bad guy'' and not the ''good guy''. <br/> <br/>Something to think about anyway.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2013-01-02 02:32:05.</i></td>', 'if anyone felt offended  not offended but my dislike of guns does affect my interaction with and interpretation of the picture. if photography is viewed as art and not just a technical process of capturing an image, then it isn''t just about the technical aspects of the photo but how the viewer interacts with it as well.  I think that there are a few examples in the comments below of how the opinions of the viewer affect the interaction.  You wanted to convey the good guy "sheriff" holding his ground against the outlaws but more than a few comments below show a completely different interpretation despite your title. It is described as ''ominous'', ''menacing'', I used ''vigilante'' and others referenced "Deliverance". This would indicate that on some level with some viewers your image failed to convey a ''good guy'' impression. Perhaps the absence of a clear ''bad guy'' in the image contributes as well as the fact that your character is wearing a black hat which image is well embedded in cowboy symbolism as belonging to the ''bad guy'' and not the ''good guy''.  Something to think about anyway.', '2013-01-02 02:29:58.000000', '2013-01-02 02:32:05.000000', 0),
(6947102, 1046326, 155989, '<td valign="top">I was one of the one''s who were less than impressed with the eye strain inducing zoom blur. The composition is interesting but honestly it was OTT with the blur which just hurt my eyes. The red reflectors in the center add interest but the really bright one on the right is totally distracting and takes your eye right away from the action forward in the picture. The best bit is the green reflectors which show motion and lead the eye forward to where the movement is going in the picture. <br/> <br/>I didn''t comment on it during voting because it was 2 am and I was tired and quite honestly my eyes hurt too much to analyse it that carefully.</td>', 'I was one of the one''s who were less than impressed with the eye strain inducing zoom blur. The composition is interesting but honestly it was OTT with the blur which just hurt my eyes. The red reflectors in the center add interest but the really bright one on the right is totally distracting and takes your eye right away from the action forward in the picture. The best bit is the green reflectors which show motion and lead the eye forward to where the movement is going in the picture.  I didn''t comment on it during voting because it was 2 am and I was tired and quite honestly my eyes hurt too much to analyse it that carefully.', '2013-01-02 02:41:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(6947111, 1046448, 155989, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Giles_uk:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by PW321:</b></div><hr/><i> Interesting concept but I''m not sure the execution was 100%. The "walker" isn''t actually walking and the brolly doesn''t fit the sunset ... no rain ... love the night cityscape. Would like to see this one done with an actual walking figure striding over the landscape which is the image your title and most of your picture suggests to me. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This is clearly shot in the dark, that''s light pollution from the city. There''s no sun setting and it was raining, and again I was walking as its a 10 second exposure I just paused longer in the middle to get some stronger definition of the silhouette<br/> <br/>//www.flickr.com/photos/cyberprop/sets/72157632348627546/ </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I was not there when you took the picture so I can''t see what you could see. I can only see what the picture suggests to me and I can''t see rain in it and the orange glow on the clouds does suggest ''sunset'' more than ''light pollution'' to me. And while you may have been walking the fact is I couldn''t see that either, I can only see the moment captured when you weren''t walking. It''s all in the eye of the beholder you see.</td>', 'Originally posted by Giles_uk: Originally posted by PW321: Interesting concept but I''m not sure the execution was 100%. The "walker" isn''t actually walking and the brolly doesn''t fit the sunset ... no rain ... love the night cityscape. Would like to see this one done with an actual walking figure striding over the landscape which is the image your title and most of your picture suggests to me.   This is clearly shot in the dark, that''s light pollution from the city. There''s no sun setting and it was raining, and again I was walking as its a 10 second exposure I just paused longer in the middle to get some stronger definition of the silhouette //www.flickr.com/photos/cyberprop/sets/72157632348627546/   I was not there when you took the picture so I can''t see what you could see. I can only see what the picture suggests to me and I can''t see rain in it and the orange glow on the clouds does suggest ''sunset'' more than ''light pollution'' to me. And while you may have been walking the fact is I couldn''t see that either, I can only see the moment captured when you weren''t walking. It''s all in the eye of the beholder you see.', '2013-01-02 02:50:25.000000', NULL, 0),
